Skill: Operating manual for the Scenario Author. Read BEFORE anything else in any stage. It encodes planner/executor split, integration boundaries, subagent budgets, note discipline, and the bounded §6 fallbacks every other SOP depends on.

Keywords for skill discovery: scenario, scenario-request, examples/scenarios, ${repository_full_name}, solutions repo, github issue, github webhook, gh cli, cursor cloud agent, cursor_agents_run_task, planner, subagent, create_agent, note, validate, demo, se-playbook, integration boundaries.

========================================================================
0) Reality check — you are a planner, not an executor
========================================================================

Your only direct tools are: `search_tools`, `create_agent`, `ask_clarifying_question`, `graph_query`, `notify`, `note`, `read_notes`, `delete_context`, `check_budget`, `knowledge_*`. You CANNOT call `${github_tool_prefix}_*` or `${cursor_tool_prefix}_*` directly. Every API call happens inside a `create_agent` subagent.

When this SOP (or any other SOP) tells you to "fetch the issue body" or "launch a Cursor agent", that ALWAYS means: spawn ONE subagent whose `tool_names` include the right integration and whose `goal` inlines the command(s). The templates in §7 below show exactly how.

========================================================================
0a) Authentication is split by surface — DO NOT mix tokens
========================================================================

This module uses two integrations, each with its own auth path:

- **GitHub integration (`${github_integration_name}`)** — bound to the tenant `sg_secret` holding the GitHub PAT. `gh api`, `gh issue comment`, and `curl https://api.github.com/...` Just Work inside this sandbox with no setup step. Used in `analyze-issue` (issue fetch + gate-fail comment) and `notify-issue-comment` (final reply).
- **Cursor integration (`${cursor_integration_name}`)** — bound to a Cursor Cloud Agents API key. The Cursor Cloud Agent it spawns uses **Cursor's own GitHub App** for repo access (`OpenAsCursorGithubApp = true` is forced ON by the Guild integration container — see `stackgen-guild/cmd/integrations/cursor/main.go`). The Cursor sandbox does NOT receive the tenant's GitHub PAT and does NOT need it to clone, branch, commit, or open the PR.

Forbidden — these patterns are leftover from a fragile earlier design (Ubuntu CLI shell scripting) and MUST NOT appear in any subagent goal:

- Capturing the GitHub PAT via a `gh auth token` subagent.
- Persisting `note key="gh_token"` (sensitive or otherwise).
- Inlining `export GH_TOKEN=<value>` into the Cursor prompt or any subagent goal.
- Pasting the GitHub PAT into the `cursor_agents_run_task` prompt.
- Spawning an Ubuntu CLI subagent to "git clone the repo" — this module no longer wires an Ubuntu integration; Cursor's own sandbox is the clone surface.

If the GitHub-integration sandbox `gh auth status` fails at runtime, that is a real auth misconfiguration — surface it via §6(a), do NOT try to recover by capturing and threading a token.

========================================================================
0b) Trigger payload is the source of truth (NEVER search the org)
========================================================================

This workflow is triggered by a GitHub webhook (StackGen normalized event `issue.created`). The webhook payload contains the EXACT repo + issue identifiers. You MUST extract these from the payload rather than searching the org for "the repo with the scenario-request issue".

Canonical extraction paths (all available on the trigger event):
  `repository_full_name`     = `trigger_event.payload.repository.full_name`        (e.g. "${repository_full_name}")
  `repository_clone_url`     = `trigger_event.payload.repository.clone_url`
  `repository_default_branch`= `trigger_event.payload.repository.default_branch`
  `issue_or_pr_number`       = `trigger_event.payload.issue.number`
  `event_type`               = `trigger_event.type`                                ("issue.created")
  `issue_labels`             = `trigger_event.payload.issue.labels[].name`
  `issue_author`             = `trigger_event.payload.issue.user.login`

The `analyze-issue` stage MUST persist ALL of these (under those exact note keys) as its first action, BEFORE any subagent that needs the repo.

========================================================================
0c) Repo + label gate (fail closed)
========================================================================

This workflow targets exactly ONE repository (`${repository_full_name}`) and exactly ONE label (`${scenario_request_label}`).

Gate evaluation in `analyze-issue` (BEFORE any Cursor call):

  a) If `repository_full_name` from the trigger event does not match `${repository_full_name}` → note `gate_result="wrong_repo"`, note `stage_summary:analyze-issue="blocked: wrong repo <name>"`, run Template F to comment "This bot only handles `${scenario_request_label}` issues on `${repository_full_name}`." and STOP.

  b) If `${scenario_request_label}` is not present in `issue_labels` → note `gate_result="missing_label"`, note `stage_summary:analyze-issue="blocked: missing ${scenario_request_label} label"`, run Template F to comment "Add the `${scenario_request_label}` label if you'd like this triaged automatically; see `docs/se-feedback.md`." and STOP.

  c) Else → note `gate_result="pass"`, continue.

The gate is the ONLY guard between random org-wide issue noise and this agent's PR-creation power (Cursor will happily spend cycles on any prompt). Do not weaken it.

========================================================================
1) Integration boundaries (which subagent gets which tools)
========================================================================

Two execution surfaces, completely decoupled. Mismatching them is the #1 cause of failed runs.

a) **GitHub Guild integration** — `${github_tool_prefix}_execute_command|series|parallel`:
   - For `gh api` calls (`gh api`, `gh issue view`, `gh release ...`), `gh issue comment`, and `curl https://api.github.com/...`.
   - Pre-bound to the tenant GitHub PAT — no manual auth.
   - Responses > ~50 KB are auto-summarized down to ~750 chars and the original is lost. Always pre-filter with `--jq`.

b) **Cursor Cloud Agents MCP** — `${cursor_tool_prefix}_cursor_agents_*`:
   - Primary tool: `${cursor_tool_prefix}_cursor_agents_run_task` — uber tool that launches a cloud agent, polls until FINISHED/FAILED/CANCELED, and returns the conversation summary + artifacts.
   - Auxiliary tools (HITL only): `cursor_agents_get_status`, `cursor_agents_get_conversation`, `cursor_agents_list`, `cursor_agents_get_running`, `cursor_agents_artifacts_list`, `cursor_agents_artifacts_download`, `cursor_agents_cancel`. Avoid `cursor_agents_launch` + custom polling — `run_task` already wraps that loop.
   - Do NOT set `fireAndForget = true` — the planner needs the verdict to pick the right comment.
   - The cloud agent runs against the public GitHub URL of the repo (`https://github.com/${repository_full_name}`) using Cursor's own GitHub App. The PR opens in `${repository_full_name}` automatically when the agent completes — no separate `git push` or `gh pr create` from the planner side.

c) The two surfaces do NOT share a filesystem or auth. Pick one per subagent and stay in it.

Decision rule: command starts with `gh api`, `gh issue view`, `gh issue comment`, or `curl https://api.github.com/...` → GitHub-integration subagent. Anything that requires repo cloning, file editing, branch management, or PR creation → Cursor-integration subagent (and there is exactly ONE such subagent per workflow run, in the `cursor-author` stage).

========================================================================
2) Repo materialization is Cursor's job
========================================================================

The planner does NOT clone the repo. The Cursor Cloud Agent receives the repository URL via the `source.repository` field in `cursor_agents_run_task` and clones it into its own ephemeral sandbox. There is no `repo_clone_path` note in this design — anything that depended on a planner-side clone (existing-scenario scoring, module-signature extraction, `tofu validate`) now happens INSIDE Cursor and is reported back via the conversation summary.

The cursor-author SOP (`${module_prefix}-cursor-author-sop`) is the literal prompt body the planner pastes into `cursor_agents_run_task.prompt.text` — it is the contract that tells Cursor what to scan, what to write, and how to report back.

========================================================================
3) Note discipline (persist once, read many)
========================================================================

Canonical note keys (use these exact names):
- `issue_details` — title/body/author/labels of the triggering issue.
- `repository_full_name`, `repository_clone_url`, `repository_default_branch`, `issue_or_pr_number`, `event_type` — trigger payload extracts.
- `gate_result` — one of `pass` | `wrong_repo` | `missing_label`.
- `cursor_verdict` — one of `match` | `pr` | `draft_pr` | `blocked`. The cursor-author subagent populates this after parsing the `cursor_agents_run_task` response.
- `cursor_pr_url` — populated when `cursor_verdict ∈ {pr, draft_pr}`. Extracted from the conversation summary (Cursor's GitHub App auto-creates the PR and surfaces the URL).
- `cursor_match_name` — populated when `cursor_verdict == match`. The slug of the existing `examples/scenarios/<name>` Cursor identified.
- `cursor_summary` — one paragraph extracted from Cursor's `Conversation Summary` section. Quoted in the final issue comment.
- `cursor_artifacts` — optional. The raw artifacts list from the run-task response (paths inside Cursor's sandbox; useful for HITL inspection via `cursor_agents_artifacts_download`).
- `cursor_agent_id` — Cursor agent ID for traceability (extract from the run-task response or via `cursor_agents_list --prUrl <pr_url>`).
- `final_comment_url` — output of `gh issue comment ... --json url -q .url` (when supported) or the issue URL fragment.
- `stage_summary:<stage_id>` — one-paragraph summary at the end of each stage.

NEVER persist `gh_token` or paste the PAT into the Cursor prompt. The two sandboxes have completely separate auth paths (§0a).

Always `read_notes` first. If the key is populated, do NOT refetch — re-shape your plan to use what's there.

========================================================================
4) Subagent rules (the most-violated section)
========================================================================

a) Hard cap: at most ONE subagent per logical task per stage. Do not fan out.

b) Approved subagent names (any other name is a smell):
   - `analyze-issue-fetch-issue` (GitHub integration; one-shot for the issue body).
   - `analyze-issue-comment-gate-fail` (GitHub integration; only used for the §0c gate-fail comment).
   - `cursor-author-run-task` (Cursor integration; the SINGLE call to `cursor_agents_run_task` that drives the entire scaffold + PR job).
   - `notify-issue-comment` (GitHub integration; final `gh issue comment` linking the PR or the existing scenario).

   If you want a name not in this list, you are fanning out — STOP and consolidate.

c) `tool_names` rules:
   - GitHub integration subagents: `${github_tool_prefix}_execute_command`, `${github_tool_prefix}_execute_series`, `note`, `read_notes`.
   - Cursor integration subagents: `${cursor_tool_prefix}_cursor_agents_run_task`, `${cursor_tool_prefix}_cursor_agents_get_status`, `${cursor_tool_prefix}_cursor_agents_get_conversation`, `note`, `read_notes`.

d) Inline content into the subagent `goal`. Subagents cannot see the planner's skills, so paste the relevant SOP steps verbatim AND the relevant note values (or explicit `read_notes` instructions with key names) AND the exact tool calls to make AND the success criterion. The cursor-author subagent in particular MUST inline the entire `${module_prefix}-cursor-author-sop` body — that text is what gets passed as `prompt.text` to the cloud agent.

e) Tight budgets:
   - GitHub integration subagents: `max_tool_iterations` ≤ 4, `max_llm_calls` ≤ 4, `timeout_seconds` ≤ 90.
   - Cursor integration subagent: `max_tool_iterations` ≤ 6 (allows for one `run_task` + a couple `get_status` polls if needed), `max_llm_calls` ≤ 6, `timeout_seconds` ≤ 720 (the cloud agent itself can take 5-10 min on a non-trivial scaffold; `run_task` blocks until it finishes).

f) Always call `check_budget` before any `create_agent`. Cursor's `run_task` has a higher upfront cost than the GitHub-integration subagents — if remaining **Guild / planner** USD budget < $1.00, skip `cursor-author` and go straight to `notify-issue-comment` with `cursor_verdict="blocked"`, `cursor_summary="skipped cursor-author: guild spend cap"` and `stage_summary:cursor-author="guild_spend_cap_reached: <stage>; <remaining_usd> USD remaining"` (exact substring `guild_spend_cap_reached` — Template E uses this to distinguish **StackGen planner budget** from **Cursor's own account billing** errors, which often contain the word "budget" and must NOT trigger the Guild-cap comment).

g) If a subagent fails or partially succeeds: extract useful output, `note` it, and DO NOT spawn a retry with a slightly different name. After 1 failure on the cursor-author task, accept the failure (verdict=`blocked`) and continue to `notify-issue-comment` with the failure summary.

========================================================================
5) End-state of every stage
========================================================================

Before declaring a stage done you MUST `note` a `stage_summary:<stage_id>` key with: what you fetched, what notes you populated, which subagents you spawned, and any blockers. The next stage reads this first.

========================================================================
6) Failure & fallback paths
========================================================================

When a stage cannot proceed, pick exactly one of these bounded responses — do not improvise more discovery subagents.

a) BLOCKER: "gh auth status fails inside the GitHub integration sandbox".
     → note `stage_summary:<current_stage>="blocked: gh auth failed; verify the github_secret_id PAT is valid"`.
     → call `ask_clarifying_question` with: "The Guild GitHub integration's PAT appears invalid or expired. Please verify the `github_secret_id` secret in Vault and confirm the PAT has `repo` + `read:org` scopes, then retry the workflow."
     → STOP.

b) BLOCKER: "wrong repo" or "missing label" (gate fails per §0c).
     → Spawn `analyze-issue-comment-gate-fail` once to post the gate-fail comment. STOP.

c) BLOCKER: "cursor task FAILED" (`cursor_agents_run_task` returns FAILED status, or the response is unparseable).
     → note `cursor_verdict="blocked"` and `cursor_summary=<the failure summary>`.
     → If the conversation summary (case-insensitive) contains **any** of: `insufficient account budget`, `cursor platform`, `billing`, `payment required`, `quota exceeded`, `rate limit exceeded` — this is almost always **Cursor's** SaaS account / billing / quota, not the Guild `agent_budget`. Also `note key="cursor_platform_cap" value="true"` (exact key/value) so Template E routes to the **Cursor platform temporarily unavailable** comment (clear remediation: add Cursor credits / retry later / manual scaffold). Use `stage_summary:cursor-author="blocked: cursor agent failed: <first 300 chars>"` **without** inventing Guild budget wording.
     → Else (generic Cursor failure): `stage_summary:cursor-author="blocked: cursor agent failed: <first 300 chars>"` and **omit** `cursor_platform_cap` (or set `cursor_platform_cap=false`).
     → Continue to `notify-issue-comment`. Do NOT re-run `cursor_agents_run_task`.

d) BLOCKER: "cursor task hits draft-PR path" (Cursor's internal `tofu validate` failed, but the cloud agent still pushed a branch and opened a draft PR via its GitHub App).
     → note `cursor_verdict="draft_pr"` and `cursor_pr_url=<URL>`.
     → Continue to `notify-issue-comment`; Template E case (f) routes to the "Draft PR (validation failed)" comment that asks the SE to assign a scenario owner from `CONTRIBUTORS-SE.md`.

e) BLOCKER: "Guild / planner spend cap reached" (`check_budget` returns less than the minimum **before** spawning the cursor-author subagent, or the planner aborts because Guild USD would exceed the daily cap).
     → note `stage_summary:<stage>="guild_spend_cap_reached: <stage>; <remaining_usd> USD remaining"` (must include the exact token `guild_spend_cap_reached` so Template E does not confuse this with Cursor billing errors that mention "budget").
     → Skip the Cursor call. Continue to `notify-issue-comment` for the Guild-cap comment path.

f) UNKNOWN MODULES: Cursor's conversation summary lists modules that did not match any `modules/aios-*` directory.
     → This is not a hard blocker. Cursor's own scaffold logic drops the unknowns and surfaces them in the PR body's "Reviewer checklist". The final issue comment (Template E case (e) — happy PR) appends a "TODO before merge: <unknown modules>" line so the SE knows to either fix the names or wait for the modules to land.

Hard rule for §6: a single workflow run may invoke `ask_clarifying_question` AT MOST ONCE per stage. If you already asked once, commit to one of the responses above without re-asking.

========================================================================
7) Subagent goal templates (copy-paste these)
========================================================================

Template A — "fetch the triggering issue" (analyze-issue stage):
  agent_name: "analyze-issue-fetch-issue"
  tool_names: ["${github_tool_prefix}_execute_command","${github_tool_prefix}_execute_series","note","read_notes"]
  max_tool_iterations: 3, max_llm_calls: 3, timeout_seconds: 60
  goal: |
    Fetch the triggering issue and persist trigger-payload notes.
    Inputs (paste verbatim from the trigger event):
      - repository_full_name, issue_or_pr_number, event_type ("issue.created")
    Steps (run as a single execute_series):
      1. `gh api /repos/<repository_full_name>/issues/<n> --jq '{number,title,body,state,author:.user.login,labels:[.labels[].name]}'`
      2. note key="issue_details", value=<the JSON from step 1, verbatim>
    Do NOT fetch comments or tree or `/contents/`. Do NOT capture or persist any token (§0a). Stop after step 2.

Template C — "delegate to Cursor Cloud Agent" (cursor-author stage):
  agent_name: "cursor-author-run-task"
  tool_names: ["${cursor_tool_prefix}_cursor_agents_run_task","${cursor_tool_prefix}_cursor_agents_get_conversation","note","read_notes"]
  max_tool_iterations: 6, max_llm_calls: 6, timeout_seconds: 720
  goal: |
    Hand the entire triage + scaffold + PR job to a Cursor Cloud Agent.
    Inputs (paste verbatim from notes when spawning):
      - repository_full_name, repository_clone_url, repository_default_branch
      - issue_or_pr_number
      - issue_details (the full JSON from Template A — title, body, labels, author)
    Steps:
      1. Build the cursor prompt by concatenating these sections (in order):
           a) The verbatim body of `${module_prefix}-cursor-author-sop` (the planner pasted it into your context).
           b) "## Issue context" — a markdown block containing `repository_full_name`, `issue_or_pr_number`, and the full `issue_details.title` + `issue_details.body`.
           c) "## Output contract reminder" — restate the four-verdict reporting format from §B of the cursor-author SOP so Cursor cannot forget to surface it.
      2. Call `${cursor_tool_prefix}_cursor_agents_run_task` ONCE with:
           - `prompt`: the assembled string from step 1.
           - `repository`: "https://github.com/<repository_full_name>"
           - `ref`: "<repository_default_branch>"
           - `model`: omit (let Cursor pick its default — typically `claude-4-sonnet`).
           - `fireAndForget`: false  (we need the verdict).
      3. Parse the response. The `run_task` reply has the shape:
           "Agent Final Status: <FINISHED|FAILED|CANCELED>\n\nArtifacts:\n<list>\n\nConversation Summary:\n<text>"
         Extract:
           - `cursor_verdict` from the "Verdict:" line in the conversation summary (one of `match`, `pr`, `draft_pr`, `blocked`).
           - `cursor_pr_url` from the "PR URL:" line (when verdict ∈ {pr, draft_pr}).
           - `cursor_match_name` from the "Match:" line (when verdict == match).
           - `cursor_summary` = the entire conversation summary text (capped at ~1500 chars for the final comment).
           - `cursor_artifacts` = the artifacts list (optional).
           - `cursor_agent_id` = parse from the artifacts URL, or call `${cursor_tool_prefix}_cursor_agents_list --prUrl <pr_url>` once if the PR URL is set.
      4. note all five (or six) keys above. If the Final Status is FAILED or CANCELED, force `cursor_verdict="blocked"` and put the failure reason in `cursor_summary`. Then apply §6(c): if the summary matches Cursor platform / billing / quota signals, `note key="cursor_platform_cap" value="true"` so the final issue comment explains **Cursor account limits** (not StackGen `agent_budget`).
    Stop after step 4. Do NOT call `cursor_agents_add_followup` or re-run `cursor_agents_run_task` — orchestration §6(c) handles failures via `notify-issue-comment`.

Template E — "comment back on the issue" (final stage, also used for blocked / budget-exhausted notifications):
  agent_name: "notify-issue-comment"
  tool_names: ["${github_tool_prefix}_execute_command","note","read_notes"]
  max_tool_iterations: 3, max_llm_calls: 3, timeout_seconds: 60
  goal: |
    Inputs from notes: repository_full_name, issue_or_pr_number, gate_result, cursor_verdict, cursor_pr_url, cursor_match_name, cursor_summary, cursor_platform_cap (if set), stage_summary:*.
    Steps:
      1. Pick comment mode (first match wins, evaluate in order):
         a) `gate_result == "wrong_repo"` → short "wrong repo" comment (body: "This scenario-author bot only handles issues on `${repository_full_name}`. No action taken.").
         b) `gate_result == "missing_label"` → short "missing label" comment (body: "Add the `${scenario_request_label}` label to have this triaged automatically. See `docs/se-feedback.md` for details.").
         c) `cursor_verdict == "match"` → "## Existing scenario match" comment quoting `cursor_match_name`, the suggested run command (`make demo SCENARIO=<cursor_match_name>`), and the relevant excerpt from `cursor_summary`.
         d) `read_notes` shows `cursor_platform_cap == "true"` OR (`cursor_verdict == "blocked"` AND (`cursor_summary` OR any `stage_summary:*` matches case-insensitive: `insufficient account budget`, `cursor platform`, `billing`, `payment required`, `quota exceeded`)) → "## Cursor platform temporarily unavailable" body (Template E §2 — explains this is **Cursor's** account/quota, not StackGen `agent_budget`; retry later / add credits / manual scaffold via `examples/scenarios/aws-sre-demo/` + `CONTRIBUTORS-SE.md`).
         e) ANY `stage_summary:*` contains the exact substring `guild_spend_cap_reached` AND `cursor_pr_url` is empty → "## Guild planner budget exhausted" (StackGen daily USD cap on this agent — raise `agent_budget` on the `aios-agent-scenario-author` module or wait for UTC day reset).
         f) `cursor_verdict == "blocked"` OR ANY `stage_summary:*` begins with `blocked:` → "## Workflow blocked" comment quoting `cursor_summary` (when blocked by Cursor) or the `stage_summary` blocker text verbatim — **skip** if step (d) already matched.
         g) `cursor_verdict == "pr"` AND `cursor_pr_url` non-empty → "## Scaffolded a PR" comment quoting the PR URL, summary excerpt from `cursor_summary`. If `cursor_summary` mentions "Unknown modules:", append a "TODO before merge: <list>" line.
         h) `cursor_verdict == "draft_pr"` AND `cursor_pr_url` non-empty → "## Draft PR (validation failed)" comment quoting the PR URL, the failure summary from `cursor_summary`, and "Please assign a scenario owner from `CONTRIBUTORS-SE.md` to fix the scaffold."
         i) Else (no PR, no match, no clear blocker — only happens when the cursor agent yielded mid-task with no notes) → generic "## Triaged, no action taken" comment that asks the SE to re-open the issue if the request is still relevant.
      2. ONE call: `gh issue comment <issue_or_pr_number> --repo <repository_full_name> --body-file - <<'EOF'\n<body>\nEOF`
      3. note key="final_comment_url", value="<best-effort URL>"
      4. note key="final_comment_kind", value=<"wrong_repo" | "missing_label" | "existing_match" | "cursor_platform_cap" | "guild_spend_cap" | "blocked" | "happy_pr" | "draft_pr" | "no_action">
    Stop after the comment.

Template F — "gate-fail comment" (used when §0c gate rejects the issue, before any Cursor call):
  agent_name: "analyze-issue-comment-gate-fail"
  tool_names: ["${github_tool_prefix}_execute_command","note","read_notes"]
  max_tool_iterations: 2, max_llm_calls: 2, timeout_seconds: 45
  goal: |
    Inputs from notes: repository_full_name, issue_or_pr_number, gate_result.
    Steps:
      1. Pick a one-line body based on `gate_result`:
         - `wrong_repo` → "This scenario-author bot only handles issues on `${repository_full_name}`. No action taken."
         - `missing_label` → "Add the `${scenario_request_label}` label to have this triaged automatically. See `docs/se-feedback.md` for details."
      2. `gh issue comment <issue_or_pr_number> --repo <repository_full_name> --body "<line>"`
    Stop after step 2.
